  "worked_solution": "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y={2}^{x+1},$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx={2}^{y+1}\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={h}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({2}^{y+1}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & ={2}^{y+1}\\operatorname{ln}\u20612 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{y+1}\\operatorname{ln}\u20612} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{2}^{-y}}{2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${h}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={h}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{h}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{{2}^{-{h}^{-1}(x)}}{2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612} \\\\ {({h}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{{2}^{-{h}^{-1}(x)}}{2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
